Geometry deduplication modes (HDARNOLD_GEOM_DEDUP) produce the expected Arnold scene

The render delegate can render geometrically identical geometries (meshes or curves) as
instances of a single canonical Arnold node instead of duplicating the geometry. This is
controlled by the HDARNOLD_GEOM_DEDUP environment variable, with three modes:
  0 = None       : every mesh keeps its own geometry;
  1 = Instances  : only point-instancer prototypes are deduplicated (the default, and the
                   case that matters - UsdImaging re-roots a copy of each instancer's
                   prototype, so identical prototypes arrive as separate polymeshes);
  2 = All        : plain non-instanced duplicates are deduplicated too (each through a
                   single-instance instancer).

This test expands the same scene once per mode (through the usd procedural's Hydra path,
where the deduplication lives) and checks the resulting Arnold scene directly, without any
image comparison: it counts polymeshes and curves that actually carry geometry, and instancer
nodes. scene.usda has parallel mesh and curve groups: two point instancers with identical
prototypes (deduplicated in modes 1 and 2), one point instancer whose prototype differs by
one point (never merged), and two identical non-instanced shapes (merged only in mode 2). The
instancer count is the total across meshes and curves: the point-instancer prototypes rendered
through an instancer node (modes 1 and 2), plus the instancer of each non-instanced duplicate
(mode 2). Expected results:
  mode 0 : 5 geometry polymeshes, 5 geometry curves, 0 instancers
  mode 1 : 4 geometry polymeshes, 4 geometry curves, 6 instancers
  mode 2 : 3 geometry polymeshes, 3 geometry curves, 8 instancers (6 + 1 mesh + 1 curve)
In every mode, no ginstance is created and no node is left without a name (a node created
with the name of one that can't be destroyed while the procedural expands is renamed to an
empty string).

The mode is read fresh from the environment on each render-delegate construction, so all
three modes can be exercised in a single process. The test links only against Arnold and
goes through the procedural (like a real render), so it never loads the render delegate's
static USD into its own process.

See ARNOLD-17180

author: sebastien.blaineau.ortega@autodesk.com
